116 | 
117 | ### 5. The most defensible triggering interpretation is late-stage localization within a segmented fault system, not a uniquely resolved direct migration path from the Mw 6.4 source to the Mw 7.1 hypocentral area
118 | 
119 | Taken together, KDE and morphology results support a nuanced interpretation:
120 | - early inter-mainshock behavior is spatially mobile but not steadily directed
121 | - late inter-mainshock behavior is more localized and less patchy on average
122 | - the geometry remains fragmented and multi-stranded throughout much of the sequence
123 | 
124 | This combination is more consistent with stress transfer and progressive activation within an existing fault network than with a single clean propagating hotspot that can be tracked continuously from the Mw 6.4 source to the Mw 7.1 nucleation point.
125 | 
126 | Support for late localization comes from:
127 | - reduced Stage 2 hotspot distance to Mw 7.1
128 | - smaller Stage 2 hotspot step lengths
129 | - much smaller high-density areas in Stage 2
130 | - frequent overlap of late KDE lobes with the corridor between the two mainshocks
131 | 
132 | Support against a simple direct migration model comes from:
133 | - alternating hotspot distances in Stage 1
134 | - persistence of two-lobed density in late Stage 2 maps
135 | - dominant fragmented morphology labels
136 | - high alpha-shape component counts across hourly windows
137 | 
138 | Evidence files:
139 | - `<PACKAGE_ROOT>/results/exp_run/outputs/01_ridgecrest_spatiotemporal_evolution/tables/stage_comparison_summary.csv`
140 | - `<PACKAGE_ROOT>/results/exp_run/outputs/01_ridgecrest_spatiotemporal_evolution/tables/kde_interval_summary.csv`
141 | - `<PACKAGE_ROOT>/results/exp_run/outputs/01_ridgecrest_spatiotemporal_evolution/tables/morphology_interval_summary.csv`
142 | - `<PACKAGE_ROOT>/results/exp_run/outputs/01_ridgecrest_spatiotemporal_evolution/tables/merged_kde_morphology_summary.csv`
